.TH TIDYFS 1 "September 2026" "tidyfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tidyfs \- conservative filesystem cleanup and disk-usage intelligence
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tidyfs
.RI [ --db " PATH" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tidyfs
indexes filesystem facts, applies deterministic cleanup policy, can request bounded read-only AI advice from an explicitly selected numeric loopback endpoint, and executes only reversible cleanup operations admitted by deterministic policy.
.PP
AI output is untrusted input. It cannot create executable cleanup candidates, lower deterministic risk, remove policy blocks, authorize mutation, or determine authoritative reclaim totals.
.PP
The default SQLite database is
.IR ~/.local/share/tidyfs/tidyfs.db .
The default quarantine root is
.IR ~/.local/share/tidyfs/quarantine/ .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --db " PATH"
Use an explicit SQLite database path. This has precedence over
.B TIDYFS_DB
and the default database path.
.TP
.B --help
Show command help.
.TP
.B --version
Show the program version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI scan " ROOT"
Scan a filesystem tree, index entries, and run deterministic classification. Use
.B --one-file-system
to avoid crossing device boundaries,
.B --include-pseudo
to include Linux pseudo-filesystems, and
.BI --jobs " N"
to select scanner concurrency.
.TP
.B classify
Run deterministic classification for an existing or latest completed scan. Use
.B --summary
to print counts by label.
.TP
.B analyze
Send a bounded metadata view of classified facts to an explicitly selected numeric loopback AI gateway. Requires
.BI --endpoint " URL" .
Optional controls include scan/root selection, risk ceiling, path disclosure mode, response-size bound, and connection/read timeouts.
.TP
.B top
Show the largest indexed directories. Optional controls include scan id, limit, relative depth, and subtree root.
.TP
.BI explain " PATH"
Explain a path from deterministic classifications and stored advisory AI evidence. Stored evidence is revalidated for freshness before use.
.TP
.B plan
Build and persist a cleanup plan from deterministic policy. Optional AI enrichment may make already-eligible candidates more conservative but cannot widen eligibility or authority.
.TP
.B recommend
Ask a numeric loopback AI gateway to select a read-only subset of an existing deterministic plan for a target reclaim size. Recommendations do not mutate the plan or authorize cleanup.
.TP
.B clean
Preview or execute allowed reversible cleanup candidates. Use
.B --dry-run
for preview. Actual cleanup requires both
.B --safe
and
.B --interactive .
.B --safe
is a dedicated mutation gate and also constrains effective cleanup risk to low; supplying
.B --risk low
without
.B --safe
does not authorize mutation.
.TP
.B adapters
List read-only tool-native cleanup adapters. Adapter findings may be reported or planned but are not executable by the raw filesystem quarantine path.
.TP
.B actions
List recorded cleanup and restore actions.
.TP
.B recover
Reconcile interrupted action state without moving or overwriting files. Select one action with
.BI --action " ID"
or all interrupted actions with
.B --all .
.TP
.B restore
Restore a quarantined action selected with
.BI --action " ID"
or
.B --latest .
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
Current pre-1.0 command output is human-oriented text unless a command documents otherwise. It is not yet a stable structured machine contract. Scripts should not depend on incidental prose formatting across minor pre-1.0 releases.
.PP
Diagnostics and recovery warnings are written to standard error. Commands do not treat piped input as approval or mutation authority.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line parsing or usage failed before command execution.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime operation failed, including filesystem, database, policy, stale-state, gateway, validation, or recovery errors.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TIDYFS_DB
Database path used when
.B --db
is not supplied.
.TP
.B HOME
Used for tilde expansion and the default state directory when no explicit database path is supplied.
.SH SAFETY AND AUTHORITY
.B tidyfs
does not permanently delete files. Reversible cleanup is serialized through a mutation lock, revalidates source identity and filesystem constraints, records transitional action state before mutation, and quarantines only admitted filesystem candidates.
.PP
.B clean
without
.B --dry-run
requires both
.B --safe
and
.B --interactive .
Neither
.B --interactive
alone nor
.B --risk low
without
.B --safe
authorizes cleanup. The explicit
.B restore
and
.B recover
commands are separate effectful operations and acquire the same mutation lock boundary.
.PP
AI endpoints are restricted to numeric loopback addresses. The AI path carries no credentials, follows no redirects, and remains advisory only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tidyfs/tidyfs.db
Default SQLite index and action database.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tidyfs/quarantine/
Default reversible quarantine storage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan a home directory without crossing filesystems:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tidyfs scan ~ --one-file-system --jobs 8
.RE
.fi
.PP
Preview only low-risk cleanup candidates:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tidyfs plan --safe
tidyfs clean --dry-run --safe
.RE
.fi
.PP
Execute the separately authorized reversible cleanup path:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tidyfs clean --safe --interactive
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inspect output through an ordinary Unix pipeline. The current output is human-oriented, so this is for interactive inspection rather than a stable machine API:
.PP
.nf
.RS
tidyfs top --limit 50 | head -n 10
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR find (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1)
.SH NOTES
The CLI is pre-1.0. Public structured-output and finer-grained exit-status contracts may be introduced in later releases; such contracts should be treated as versioned interfaces once declared stable.
